Site Setup Guide (Web UI)

This guide walks through setting up a complete site configuration from scratch using
the Web UI. By the end, you'll have a site receiving IRC announces, applying rules,
and routing files to other sites.

===============================================================================
Prerequisites
===============================================================================

Before starting:
- slftp.ini configured with [api] section enabled
- slftp binary running (./slftp or slftp.exe)
- Web UI deployed to web/ folder and accessible at http://host:port/
- IRC network accessible with site bot announcing in a channel
- Access to site credentials (user, password, host:port)

If you haven't set up the Web UI yet, see docs/WEB_UI_HOWTO.

===============================================================================
Overview
===============================================================================

Setup steps:
1. Create the site (credentials, connection)
2. Define sections and directories (ISOGAMES -> /GAMES/, MP3 -> /MP3/, etc.)
3. Add IRC network and channel (where site bot announces)
4. Configure precatcher rules (catch NEWDIR announces from site bot)
5. Add trading rules (what to allow/drop based on release properties)
6. Add routes (which sites trade to which other sites)
7. Enable auto mode (start racing)

===============================================================================
1) Create the Site
===============================================================================

Web UI: Sites Manager -> Add Site

Required fields:
- Site name: Short identifier (e.g., SITE1, FASTSITE)
- Username: FTP username for this site
- Password: FTP password
- Host:Port: FTP server address (e.g., 192.168.1.10:1234)

Optional:
- Additional BNCs: If site has multiple bounce IPs, add them after creation
  (use the site detail page to add extra BNC addresses)

After saving, verify the site appears in the Sites list.

Tip: Test connection by checking site status in the Web UI. If connection fails,
     verify credentials and network connectivity.

===============================================================================
2) Define Sections and Directories
===============================================================================

Web UI: Sites -> [click site name] -> Sections tab

What are sections?
- Sections are logical categories (MP3, TV, XVID, 0DAY, etc.)
- Each section maps to a physical directory on the FTP site
- Section names MUST match slftp.precatcher configuration

Common section examples:
- Section: ISOGAMES  -> Directory: /GAMES/
- Section: MP3       -> Directory: /MP3/
- Section: TV-X264   -> Directory: /TV-X264/
- Section: XVID      -> Directory: /XVID/
- Section: 0DAY      -> Directory: /0DAY/

Add each section your site supports:
1. Enter section name (match precatcher defaults, e.g., ISOGAMES)
2. Enter directory path (e.g., /GAMES/)
3. Click Add

Important notes:
- If a section is not defined on a site, slftp will skip racing releases there
- Directory paths typically start and end with / (e.g., /MP3/)
- Section names are case-sensitive and should match precatcher exactly

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
How Section Processing Works (slftp.precatcher)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The precatcher determines the section for each release in TWO sequential steps.
Understanding these steps helps configure your setup correctly.

Step 1: Section Detection ([sections] block)
---------------------------------------------

The precatcher searches the cleaned IRC announce text for section aliases
defined in the [sections] block.

Format:
  SECTION_NAME=alias1,alias2,alias3

Example:
  MP3=MP3,MUSIC

How it works:
- IRC announce: "( MP3 ) . Artist-Album-2025-GROUP . in /MP3/"
- After cleaning: "MP3 Artist Album 2025 GROUP in MP3"
- FindSection() searches for "MP3" or "MUSIC" in cleaned text
- Match found → Section = "MP3"
- No match → Section remains empty (not fatal, mapping step may fill it)

More examples:
  ISOGAMES=ISOGAMES,ISO GAMES,GAMES ISO,GAMES
  0DAY=0DAY,0 DAY,0 DAYS,0DAYS
  XVID=XVID,DIVX

Note: Some aliases contain spaces (e.g., "ISO GAMES") because the precatcher
converts certain characters (like "-") to spaces during text cleaning.

Key points:
- Search is case-insensitive
- Requires full word match
- First matching alias wins
- If no match found, section = empty string (continues to Step 2)

Step 2: Section Mapping ([mappings] block)
-------------------------------------------

After section detection, the precatcher applies mapping rules to remap the
section based on release name patterns. ALL mappings are processed in order,
and the first matching mapping wins.

There are THREE types of mapping entries:

Type 1: Global Mappings (applies to any section)
-------------------------------------------------

Format:
  ;TARGET_SECTION;/regex_pattern/i

The semicolon at the start creates an empty source section field, making
this a "global mapping" that applies to ANY release.

IMPORTANT: The semicolon (;) is NOT a comment character! Only # and // are
comments. Lines starting with ; are ACTIVE mappings.

Examples:
  ;FLAC;/(-FLAC-)/i
  ;EBOOK;/[._-](EBOOK)[._-]/i
  ;CHARTS;/(-(MCG)$)/i

How it works:
- Checks ALL releases during first mapping pass
- If release name matches regex → Map to target section
- Example: Release "Artist-Album-2025-FLAC-GROUP"
  Matches: ;FLAC;/(-FLAC-)/
  Result: Section = FLAC (even if Step 1 found nothing)

Use cases:
- Detecting sections from release name when not announced in IRC
- Catching releases by group name (e.g., MVID groups)
- Fallback section detection when [sections] aliases don't match

Type 2: Specific Mappings (applies only to specific source section)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

Format (Regex):
  SOURCE_SECTION;TARGET_SECTION;/regex_pattern/i

Format (Wildcard):
  SOURCE_SECTION;TARGET_SECTION;mask1,mask2,mask3

These mappings only apply if the current section matches SOURCE_SECTION.

Examples - Language-based remapping:
  EBOOK;EBOOK-DE;/[._](German)[._]/i
  EBOOK;EBOOK-ES;/[._](SPAN[iI]SH|ES)[._]/
  0DAY;0DAY-FR;/[._](FRENCH|TRUEFRENCH|FR)[._]/i

How it works:
- Only processed if current section = SOURCE_SECTION
- Example: Release "Some.Book.German.EBOOK-GROUP"
  Step 1: Detected as EBOOK (via [sections])
  Step 2: Matches EBOOK;EBOOK-DE;/[._](German)[._]/i
  Result: Section remapped to EBOOK-DE

Examples - Resolution-based remapping:
  SPORTS-SD;SPORTS-720P;/(720p)/i
  SPORTS-SD;SPORTS-1080P;/(1080p)/i

How it works:
- Release detected as SPORTS-SD in Step 1
- If name contains "720p" → Remap to SPORTS-720P
- If name contains "1080p" → Remap to SPORTS-1080P

Examples - Category-based remapping (wildcards):
  ISOGAMES;CLONEGAMES;*clone*,*clonedvd*,*clones*
  ISOGAMES;MACGAMES;*MAC*,*MACOSX*,*OSX*,*APPLE*

How it works:
- Release detected as ISOGAMES in Step 1
- If name contains "clone" wildcard → Remap to CLONEGAMES
- If name contains "MAC" wildcard → Remap to MACGAMES

Wildcard vs Regex:
- Wildcard: *pattern* (simpler, glob-style matching)
- Regex: /pattern/i (Free Pascal TRegExpr, powerful pattern matching)

Type 3: Recursive Mappings
---------------------------

If recursive_mapping is enabled in config, mapped sections can be mapped again.

Example:
  ;SPORTS-SD;/^(Football|Hockey)/i
  SPORTS-SD;SPORTS-720P;/(720p)/i

How it works:
- Release "Football.Match.720p.x264-GROUP"
- Global mapping matches → SPORTS-SD
- Specific mapping matches → SPORTS-720P (recursive)

Comment Syntax in slftp.precatcher
-----------------------------------

IMPORTANT: Understanding comment syntax is critical to avoid configuration errors.

Valid comment characters:
- # → Line comment (example: # This is a comment)
- // → Line comment (example: // This is also a comment)

NOT a comment character:
- ; → This is a FIELD SEPARATOR in mapping format, NOT a comment marker!

Examples from production config:
  # This is a comment line
  // This is also a comment line

  [sections]
  MP3=MP3,MUSIC

  [mappings]
  ;FLAC;/(-FLAC-)/i                              ← NOT a comment! Global mapping
  ;EBOOK;/[._-](EBOOK)[._-]/i                    ← NOT a comment! Global mapping
  EBOOK;EBOOK-DE;/[._](German)[._]/i             ← Specific mapping
  # ;OLD;/pattern/                               ← This IS a comment (starts with #)

Common mistake:
Users often think `;SECTION;/regex/` lines are "commented out" and inactive.
This is WRONG. These lines are ACTIVE global mappings that are processed
during the mapping phase.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Processing Order
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

When precatcher receives IRC announce, processing happens in this order:

1. Text Cleaning ([replace] block)
   - Strip colors, formatting
   - Apply replacements (e.g., "-" → space, "(" → "", etc.)
   - Remove control characters

2. Release Name Extraction
   - Find longest phrase with at least one "-" character
   - This is the release name

3. Step 1: Section Detection (FindSection function)
   - Search [sections] aliases in cleaned IRC text
   - Case-insensitive, full word match
   - Returns first matching section name or empty string

4. Step 2: Section Mapping (PrecatcherSectionMapping function)
   - Apply [mappings] rules to current section
   - Process ALL mappings in order:
     a. Global mappings (;TARGET;/regex/) checked on first pass
     b. Specific mappings (SOURCE;TARGET;/regex/) if section matches SOURCE
     c. First matching mapping wins
   - Supports recursive mapping if enabled

5. Validation
   - If section still empty and event is not COMPLETE/NUKE → Error: "No section?!"
   - Error message shows sitename and release name

6. Send to Knowledge Base
   - KB receives: sitename, section, release name, event type
   - KB triggers rule evaluation and transfers

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Practical Configuration Guide
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario 1: Add new section (FLAC) - Not announced in IRC
----------------------------------------------------------

Problem: FLAC releases are not announced as "FLAC" in IRC, but "-FLAC-" appears
in the release name.

Solution:
  1. Add global mapping to slftp.precatcher [mappings]:
     ;FLAC;/(-FLAC-)/i

  2. Add section to site (Web UI):
     Section: FLAC, Directory: /FLAC/

How it works:
- Step 1 (Detection): No match in [sections], section = empty
- Step 2 (Mapping): Global mapping ;FLAC;/(-FLAC-)/ checks ALL releases
  Release "Artist-Album-2025-FLAC-GROUP" matches → Section = FLAC
- Result: Release processed with FLAC section

Alternative (if "FLAC" IS announced in IRC):
  1. Add to [sections]: FLAC=FLAC,LOSSLESS
  2. Add section to site
  (Global mapping not needed in this case)

Scenario 2: Split section by language (German ebooks)
------------------------------------------------------

Goal: Separate German ebooks from general ebooks

Solution:
  1. Ensure EBOOK detection works:
     Option A: Add to [sections]: EBOOK=EBOOK,EBOOKS,E-BOOK
     Option B: Add global mapping: ;EBOOK;/[._-](EBOOK)[._-]/i
     Option C: Both (recommended for reliability)

  2. Add language-specific mapping to [mappings]:
     EBOOK;EBOOK-DE;/[._](German|DE)[._]/i

  3. Add both sections to site (Web UI):
     Section: EBOOK, Directory: /EBOOK/
     Section: EBOOK-DE, Directory: /EBOOK-DE/

How it works:
- Release "Some.Book.EBOOK-GROUP"
  Step 1: Detected as EBOOK
  Step 2: No language match → Stays EBOOK
  Result: Goes to /EBOOK/

- Release "Some.Book.German.EBOOK-GROUP"
  Step 1: Detected as EBOOK
  Step 2: Matches EBOOK;EBOOK-DE → Remapped to EBOOK-DE
  Result: Goes to /EBOOK-DE/

Scenario 3: Split section by resolution (TV shows)
---------------------------------------------------

Goal: Separate TV shows by resolution (720p vs 1080p)

Solution:
  1. Ensure TV section exists:
     Add to [sections]: TV-X264=TV-X264,TV X264,TVX264

  2. Add resolution mappings to [mappings]:
     TV-X264;TV-720P;/(720p)/i
     TV-X264;TV-1080P;/(1080p)/i

  3. Add sections to site (Web UI):
     Section: TV-720P, Directory: /TV-720P/
     Section: TV-1080P, Directory: /TV-1080P/

How it works:
- Release "Show.Name.S01E01.720p.x264-GROUP"
  Step 1: Detected as TV-X264
  Step 2: Matches /(720p)/ → Remapped to TV-720P
  Result: Goes to /TV-720P/

Scenario 4: Catch releases by group name (MVID)
------------------------------------------------

Goal: Catch music video releases based on group names, not IRC announce

Solution:
  1. Add global mapping to slftp.precatcher [mappings]:
     ;MVID;/(-(GRMV|SRP|VFi|SNO|PmV)$)/i

  2. Add section to site (Web UI):
     Section: MVID, Directory: /MVID/

How it works:
- Release "Artist-Video-2025-SRP"
  Step 1: No match in [sections], section = empty
  Step 2: Global mapping matches /(-(SRP)$)/ → Section = MVID
  Result: Processed as MVID

Why global mapping?
- MVID is not typically announced in IRC text
- Group names are the reliable indicator
- Global mapping catches it from release name

Scenario 5: Combine detection and mapping (comprehensive)
----------------------------------------------------------

Goal: Robust SPORTS section handling with resolution splitting

Solution:
  1. Add to [sections]:
     SPORTS=SPORTS,SPORT,FOOTBALL,HOCKEY,BASEBALL

  2. Add global mapping as fallback:
     ;SPORTS-SD;/^(Football|Hockey|Baseball|NBA|NFL)/i

  3. Add resolution mappings:
     SPORTS-SD;SPORTS-720P;/(720p)/i
     SPORTS-SD;SPORTS-1080P;/(1080p)/i

  4. Add sections to site:
     Section: SPORTS-720P, Directory: /SPORTS-720P/
     Section: SPORTS-1080P, Directory: /SPORTS-1080P/

How it works:
- Release "Football.Match.720p.x264-GROUP"
  Step 1: May or may not match [sections] SPORTS alias
  Step 2a: If not matched, global mapping catches it → SPORTS-SD
  Step 2b: Resolution mapping matches → SPORTS-720P
  Result: Goes to /SPORTS-720P/ (reliable even if IRC doesn't announce "SPORTS")

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Troubleshooting
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Problem: "No section?!" error in logs
--------------------------------------

Error message:
  "Section on SITENAME for Release-Name-GROUP was not found. Add Sectionname
   to slftp.precatcher under [sections] and/or [mappings]."

Causes:
- Step 1: No [sections] alias matched in IRC announce
- Step 2: No global mapping matched release name
- Result: Section remains empty

Solutions:
  Option A: Add alias to [sections]
    Example: FLAC=FLAC,LOSSLESS

  Option B: Add global mapping to [mappings]
    Example: ;FLAC;/(-FLAC-)/i

  Option C: Both (recommended)
    Provides redundancy if IRC announce format changes

Problem: Wrong section detected
--------------------------------

Cause:
- Multiple [sections] aliases overlap
- Example: SPORTS=SPORTS,SPORT and SPORTS-4K=SPORTS-4K,SPORT-4K
- IRC announces "SPORT" → First match wins

Solutions:
  1. Make aliases more specific
     Before: SPORTS=SPORTS,SPORT
     After: SPORTS=SPORTS (remove generic "SPORT")

  2. Use mapping to fix after detection
     Add: SPORTS;SPORTS-4K;/(2160p|4K)/i

  3. Rely on global mappings instead of [sections]
     Remove from [sections], use ;SPORTS;/pattern/ in [mappings]

Problem: Mapping not working
----------------------------

Checklist:
  1. Verify mapping is NOT commented
     ✓ Correct: ;FLAC;/(-FLAC-)/i
     ✗ Wrong: # ;FLAC;/(-FLAC-)/i (commented with #)

  2. Verify semicolon count = 2
     ✓ Correct: ;SECTION;/regex/i (2 semicolons)
     ✗ Wrong: SECTION;/regex/i (1 semicolon, invalid format)

  3. Test regex syntax
     Use online regex tester with release name
     Ensure regex is TRegExpr compatible (Free Pascal regex engine)

  4. Check source section (for specific mappings)
     Example: EBOOK;EBOOK-DE;/German/
     Only works if current section = "EBOOK"
     If section is empty or different, mapping is skipped

  5. Check mapping order
     Mappings are processed in order, first match wins
     If earlier mapping already remapped section, later ones may not apply

Problem: Section exists but releases not racing
------------------------------------------------

Checklist:
  1. Section added to destination site (Web UI)?
     Check: Sites -> [site] -> Sections
     Must have matching section name

  2. Routes configured between sites?
     Check: Routes page
     Need bidirectional routes for trading

  3. Rules dropping release?
     Check: Logs page for rule evaluation
     Use Rules test tool to debug

  4. Precatcher catching announces?
     Check: IRC -> Channels -> Precatcher tab
     Verify bot nick, event type, words filter

Config reload after changes
----------------------------

After editing slftp.precatcher, you must reload the configuration:

Method 1: IRC command (preferred)
  !catchreload

Method 2: Restart slftp
  Stop and restart the slftp binary

Note: The Web UI does not have a reload button for precatcher config.
      You must use IRC or restart.

===============================================================================
3) Add IRC Network and Channel
===============================================================================

Step 3a: Add IRC Network
-------------------------
Web UI: IRC -> Networks -> Add Network

Fields:
- Network name: Short identifier (e.g., LINKNET, EFNet)
- Host: IRC server address (e.g., irc.link-net.org)
- Port: IRC server port (e.g., 7000 for SSL, 6667 for plain)
- SSL: Enable if server supports SSL/TLS (recommended)
- Password: Server/ZNC password (for ZNC: user:pass or user/network:pass)
- Nick: IRC nickname (defaults to slftp.ini setting if empty)
- Ident: IRC identity/realname (defaults to config + "@soulless.ftp")
- User: IRC username (defaults to slftp.ini setting if empty)

Example (direct connection):
  Network: LINKNET
  Host: eu.link-net.org:7000
  SSL: Yes

Example (ZNC bouncer):
  Network: MyBNC
  Host: bouncer.example.com:1337
  SSL: Yes
  User: myznc:mypassword
  (For multiple ZNC networks: myznc/linknet:mypassword)

After saving, check connection status in IRC -> Networks list.

Step 3b: Add IRC Channel
-------------------------
Web UI: IRC -> Channels -> Add Channel

Fields:
- Network: Select network from dropdown
- Channel: Channel name (e.g., #sitechan)
- Key: Channel password (if required)
- Blowfish: Encryption key (if channel uses +CBC encryption)
- Roles: Space or comma-separated roles (see below)

Blowfish notes:
- For CBC encryption, prefix key with "cbc:" (e.g., cbc:secretkey123)
- For admin channels, blowfish is REQUIRED
- If using ZNC with fish, you may skip this (ZNC handles encryption)

IRC Channel Roles (critical field!):

Common roles:
- ADMIN: Required for admin commands (addsite, catchadd, rules) - admin channel only
- STATS: Announces race stats and KB hits
- ERROR: Announces all error messages (useful for debugging)
- KB: Allows manual KB commands (lookups, additions)
- ADDPRE: Required for pretime checking (set on pre bot channels)
- PRECATCHSTATS: Shows detailed race progress

Additional roles:
- NUKE: Allows manual nuke commands
- GROUP: Allows group commands (pre, spread, batch, check)
- INFO: Announces IMDB/TVMaze metadata fetch results
- ADDIMDB, ADDTVMAZE, ADDGN: Parse metadata from IRC posts
- ADDIMDBECHO, ADDTVMAZEECHO, ADDGNECHO: Echo metadata to help other clients
- RACESTATS, RANKSTATS, SPEEDSTATS: Detailed performance announcements
- ROUTEINFOS: Route order announcements during races
- SKIPLOG: Announces skipped files/dirs
- IRCEVENT: Announces NICK/KICK/JOIN/TOPIC/PART events
- INDEXER: Announces autoindexer process messages

See docs/ircchanroles_description for complete list and detailed descriptions.

Important: If you want slftp to accept admin commands, at least one channel
           must have the ADMIN role set.

===============================================================================
4) Add Precatcher Rules (Catch Announces)
===============================================================================

Web UI: IRC -> Channels -> [click channel] -> Precatcher tab

What is the precatcher?
- Monitors IRC channel for site bot announces
- Extracts release name, section, event type (NEWDIR, COMPLETE, etc.)
- Sends parsed info to Knowledge Base (KB) which triggers rules/transfers

How it works:
When site bot announces: "( MP3 ) . Artist-Album-2025-GROUP . in /MP3/"

Precatcher:
1. Strips colors/formatting
2. Extracts release name: Artist-Album-2025-GROUP
3. Identifies section: MP3 (matches [sections] in slftp.precatcher)
4. Detects event: NEWDIR (default) or COMPLETE (if "complete"/"done" in announce)
5. Sends to KB for rule evaluation

Adding a precatcher entry:
- Network: IRC network where announces come from
- Channel: IRC channel on that network
- Site: Select site this announce is for
- Bot Nicks: IRC nick(s) of site bot (comma-separated, e.g., SiteBot,SITE1)
- Event: Usually NEWDIR (recommended), or PRE/ADDPRE/COMPLETE/NUKE
- Words: Comma-separated keywords in IRC announce (e.g., NEW,RACE,MKDIR,COMPLETE)

Example:
  Network: LINKNET
  Channel: #sitechan
  Site: SITE1
  Bot Nicks: SITE1Bot
  Event: NEWDIR
  Words: NEW,RACE,MKDIR,COMPLETE

The "words" field filters IRC announces. These are keywords that appear in the
actual IRC announce text (like "NEW", "RACE"), NOT section names. If any word
matches, the line is processed by the precatcher.

Testing precatcher:
Use the Test tool in the Precatcher tab (equivalent to !catchtest command).
Paste a real announce line and verify:
- Release name extracted correctly
- Section identified
- Event detected

Example test:
Input: "( MP3 ) . Artist-Album-2025-GROUP . in /MP3/"
Output:
  Release: Artist-Album-2025-GROUP
  Section: MP3
  Event: NEWDIR
  Result: OK

Troubleshooting:
- "No section?!" error: Section not defined in slftp.precatcher [sections]
  Solution: Add section alias in slftp.precatcher or use different section name

- Release not caught: Check "words" filter, ensure at least one word matches

- Wrong section detected: Check section aliases in slftp.precatcher [sections]
  Overlapping aliases (e.g., "TV" and "TV-X264") might conflict

Important: If a section is not defined in slftp.precatcher, precatcher will NOT
           send events for that section to KB, even if the site has that section.

===============================================================================
5) Add Trading Rules
===============================================================================

Web UI: Rules -> Add Rule

What are rules?
- Rules filter releases based on properties (group, year, genre, country, etc.)
- Applied per site + section combination
- Decide whether to ALLOW or DROP a release

Rule evaluation:
- Rules evaluated in order (rule #1, #2, #3, ...)
- First matching ALLOW rule wins (release allowed)
- If no ALLOW matches, release is dropped
- Exception: Global rules (site=*, section=*) always evaluated first

Rule syntax:
  if <condition> then ALLOW/DROP

Available conditions (use !rulelist or Web UI help):
- default: Always true (catch-all)
- group: Release group (e.g., group = INTERNAL, group in GRP1, GRP2)
- releasename: Release name mask (e.g., releasename =~ *PROPER*)
- age: Release age in seconds (e.g., age > 3600)
- internal: Internal release flag
- mp3year, mp3genre, mp3language, mp3source, mp3numdisks: MP3 metadata
- tvlookupdone, tvcountry, tvscripted: TV metadata (requires TVMaze lookup)
- imdblookupdone, imdbyear, imdbrating: IMDB metadata

Operators:
- = : Equal
- != : Not equal
- @ : Site name equality (e.g., site @ SITE1)
- =~ : Mask match (*wildcard*) or regex (/pattern/)
- !~ : Not mask/regex match
- in : List match (e.g., group in GRP1, GRP2, GRP3)
- notin : Not in list
- < <= >= > : Numeric comparison

Rule strategy:
1. Start with DROP rules (blacklist)
2. Add specific ALLOW rules (whitelist)
3. End with default DROP or ALLOW

Example 1: Allow everything in XVID section
  Site: SITE1
  Section: XVID
  Rule: if default then ALLOW

Example 2: MP3 rules (year >= 2009, CD/Vinyl only, English, no bootlegs)
  # Drop old releases (pretime > 60 minutes)
  Rule 1: if age > 3600 then DROP

  # Drop failure groups
  Rule 2: if group in EOSINT, FiH then DROP

  # Drop internal releases
  Rule 3: if internal then DROP

  # Drop non-English
  Rule 4: if not mp3language in en then DROP

  # Drop bad sources
  Rule 5: ifnot mp3source in CD, CDR, Vinyl, WEB then DROP

  # Drop bootlegs
  Rule 6: if mp3type in audiobook, bootleg then DROP

  # Drop old releases
  Rule 7: if mp3year < 2009 then DROP

  # Allow everything else
  Rule 8: if default then ALLOW

Example 3: TV rules (USA/UK scripted shows only)
  # Wait for TVMaze lookup before evaluating
  Rule 1: ifnot tvlookupdone then DROP

  # Drop non-USA/UK shows
  Rule 2: if tvcountry notin USA, UK then DROP

  # Drop non-scripted (reality TV, etc.)
  Rule 3: ifnot tvscripted then DROP

  # Allow everything else
  Rule 4: if default then ALLOW

Important: Lookup-dependent rules
For TV/IMDB/MP3 lookups to work, configure [kb] section in slftp.ini:
  TMP3Release=MP3,FLAC
  TTVRelease=TV,TV-X264,TV-XVID
  TIMDBRelease=XVID,X264,DVDR,BLURAY

Use *lookupdone rules to prevent early drops:
- tvlookupdone: Wait for TVMaze lookup
- imdblookupdone: Wait for IMDB lookup
- mvidlookupdone: Wait for music video lookup

Without these, releases will be dropped before metadata is available.

When lookup completes, an UPDATE event is fired which re-evaluates rules with
full metadata.

Testing rules:
Use the Test tool in Rules page to simulate rule evaluation on an existing release.

===============================================================================
6) Add Routes
===============================================================================

Web UI: Routes -> Add Route

What are routes?
- Routes define which sites transfer to which other sites
- Each route has a weight (1-9, higher = preferred)
- No route = no transfer (even if rules allow it)

Route properties:
- Source site: Where files come from
- Destination site: Where files go to
- Weight: Initial priority (0-9)
  9 = highest priority (try first)
  1 = lowest priority (backup route)
  0 = deletes the route

Dynamic routing:
slftp automatically adjusts route weights based on:
- Transfer speed (faster routes get higher weight)
- Success rate (failing routes get lower weight)
- Site ranking (site performance over time)

See docs/chains for detailed routing algorithm explanation.

Example setup (3 sites: SITE1, SITE2, SITE3):
  Route 1: SITE1 -> SITE2 (weight 9)
  Route 2: SITE1 -> SITE3 (weight 7)
  Route 3: SITE2 -> SITE1 (weight 9)
  Route 4: SITE2 -> SITE3 (weight 8)
  Route 5: SITE3 -> SITE1 (weight 8)
  Route 6: SITE3 -> SITE2 (weight 9)

This creates a full mesh where every site can transfer to every other site.

Bidirectional routes:
To set up bidirectional routing (SITE1 <-> SITE2), add both directions:
  SITE1 -> SITE2 (weight 9)
  SITE2 -> SITE1 (weight 9)

Strategy:
- Start simple (full mesh with weight 9)
- Run speedtests (Web UI: Speedtest page)
- Adjust weights based on results (or let slftp auto-adjust)

Important: Without routes, files won't transfer even if rules allow them.

===============================================================================
7) Enable Auto Mode (Precatcher Auto)
===============================================================================

Web UI: Header (top right) -> Auto switch

What is Precatcher Auto mode?
- When disabled: slftp catches IRC announces but doesn't automatically process them
- When enabled: slftp automatically processes caught announces through rules and KB

Note: This controls the precatcher auto processing, not file transfers themselves.
Transfers still require matching rules and routes to be configured.

Before enabling auto:
1. Verify precatcher is working (test with Test tool)
2. Verify rules are correct (test with existing releases)
3. Verify routes are set up
4. Check Logs page for any errors

When you enable auto:
- New announces will trigger rule evaluation
- Matching releases will start transferring according to routes
- Stats will appear in Races page

===============================================================================
8) Verification and Monitoring
===============================================================================

After enabling auto, monitor these pages:

Logs page:
- Check for connection errors
- Check for precatcher parse errors
- Check for rule evaluation errors

Issues page:
- Shows failed transfers
- Shows connection problems
- Shows duplicate/nuke warnings

Races page:
- Shows active transfers
- Shows completed races
- Shows race statistics

Stats page:
- Shows site performance over time
- Shows transfer speeds
- Shows success rates

IRC channel (with STATS role):
- Real-time race stats
- KB hit announcements
- Route info announcements

Troubleshooting common issues:

Issue: Announces caught but no transfers
- Check routes exist between sites
- Check rules allow the release (test with Rules page)
- Check destination site has the section configured
- Check Logs page for errors

Issue: Wrong section detected
- Check slftp.precatcher [sections] aliases
- Test with Precatcher test tool
- Adjust section aliases or add mapping

Issue: Releases dropped by rules
- Check rule order (DROP rules before ALLOW rules)
- Check for *lookupdone rules (tvlookupdone, imdblookupdone)
- Test rules with Rules test tool
- Check [kb] section in slftp.ini for lookup sections

Issue: Site connection fails
- Verify credentials (username, password)
- Verify host:port is correct
- Check network connectivity
- Check site is online (test with FTP client)

===============================================================================
Advanced Topics
===============================================================================

Admin Network Setup:
If you want to use IRC commands (!addsite, !catchadd, etc.) in addition to
Web UI, set up an admin network:
- Choose network with "fakelag" disabled or OPER privileges
- Add network and channel with ADMIN role
- Set blowfish encryption (required for admin channel)
- Use !help in channel to see available commands

See docs/usage for full IRC command reference.

Section Mapping:
Remap sections based on release name patterns.
Edit slftp.precatcher [mappings]:
  ISOGAMES;CLONEGAMES;*clone*,*clonedvd*
  ISOGAMES;MACGAMES;*MAC*,*MACOSX*,*OSX*
  X264;HD720;*.720p.*
  X264;HD1080;*.1080p.*

Speedtest:
Run speedtests to optimize routes:
Web UI: Speedtest page
- Local Speedtest: Tests a single site's local speed
- Outbound (Site-to-Site): Tests transfers from one source to multiple destinations
- Inbound (Site-to-Site): Tests transfers from multiple sources to one destination
- Matrix Test: Tests all site pair combinations
- Cleanup: Cleanup test tasks
- Results update route weights automatically

Ranking:
slftp tracks site performance (speed, reliability) and adjusts internal ranking.
Sites with better ranking get prioritized in route selection.
See docs/chains for ranking algorithm details.

